            <ArticleTitle>[Coronary embolism and myocardial infarction with heat rupture during infectious endocarditis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>175-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of transmural myocardial infarction due to coronary embolism complicating infective endocarditis in a 41 year old woman. The infarction, clinically silent, was followed by rupture of left ventricular free wall, leading to hemopericardium and cardiac tamponade. The case is an uncommon complication of infective endocarditis, at the same time an uncommon cause of myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
